    "answer": {
      "short": "$\\operatorname{GCD}(km,kn)=k\\operatorname{GCD}(m,n)$ and $\\operatorname{LCM}(km,kn)=k\\operatorname{LCM}(m,n)$.\n\nFor each prime, multiplication by $k$ adds the same exponent to both numbers, so their minimum and maximum exponents each increase by that exponent.",
      "worked": "For any prime $p$, let its exponents in $k,m,n$ be $r,s,t$, respectively, allowing exponent $0$ when the prime is absent. Its exponents in $km$ and $kn$ are $r+s$ and $r+t$.\n\nThe exponent of $p$ in the GCD is\n$$\\begin{align*}\\min(r+s,r+t)&=r+\\min(s,t).\\end{align*}$$\nThis is also its exponent in $k\\operatorname{GCD}(m,n)$. Unique prime factorisation therefore gives\n$$\\operatorname{GCD}(km,kn)=k\\operatorname{GCD}(m,n).$$\nThe exponent of $p$ in the LCM is\n$$\\begin{align*}\\max(r+s,r+t)&=r+\\max(s,t).\\end{align*}$$\nThis is also its exponent in $k\\operatorname{LCM}(m,n)$. Unique prime factorisation therefore gives\n$$\\operatorname{LCM}(km,kn)=k\\operatorname{LCM}(m,n).$$",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 100
  },
